But `ConsumeIntegralInRange<size_t>` uses a variable-length encoding. Let me look at the implementation:

```cpp
template <typename T>
T ConsumeIntegralInRange(T min, T max) {
  static_assert(std::is_integral<T>::value, "An integral type is required.");
  static_assert(sizeof(T) <= sizeof(uint64_t), "Unsupported integral type.");

  if (min > max)
    abort();

  // Use the biggest type possible to hold the range and result.
  uint64_t range = static_cast<uint64_t>(max) - static_cast<uint64_t>(min);
  uint64_t result = 0;
  size_t offset = 0;

  while (offset < sizeof(T) * 8 && (range >> offset) > 0) {
    result = (result << 8) | ConsumeIntegral<uint8_t>();
    offset += 8;
  }

  if (range != std::numeric_limits<T>::max())
    result = result % (range + 1);

  return static_cast<T>(result + min);
}
```

So `ConsumeIntegralInRange<size_t>(0, max_length)` consumes a variable number of bytes depending on the range. For `max_length = 1337`, the range is 1337, which needs 2 bytes (since 1337 > 255). So it consumes 2 bytes for the size.

But the test shows that `ConsumeRandomLengthString(1337)` returns 32 bytes. If the size encoding consumes 2 bytes, then the total consumed would be 2 + 32 = 34 bytes. But the test doesn't show the total consumed, just the returned string.

